From ce37c6eeb49be1d65ee7c38f86068bd92af824b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bradley Gleave <bradley@bradleytgpcoaching.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 19:37:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(scout): roster and entities readers resolve source
 tokens through the registry (S11-E)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Staging keeps the source's own step token and the reconstruct engine plans and
ledgers each row under that same (source_platform, entity_type) group, so the
IMPORTER-G roster reader and the IMPORTER-I entities reader — which filtered
entity_type == <canonical family> — returned staged 0 / persons [] for every
source whose roster token is not literally the family name (S11-D r4 leg B
finding). That is a silent zero for a run that staged rows and a violation of
NEW SOURCE -> CORE DIFF = 0.

Both readers now classify the run's staged and ledger (platform, token) groups
through the SAME registry the engine uses (buildSourceMapperRegistry +
resolveFamily, the S10-C one classifier) and select rows by the pairs that
classify to the requested family:

- src/scout/scout-family-scope.ts (new, pure): classifyFamilyScope /
  familyScopeWhere / inFamilyScope. Legacy token == family keeps working
  (resolveFamily honours it); another family's rows are excluded from every
  count; a group no spec can classify is counted, never served, never zero.
- scout-roster.service.ts: two bounded tenant-scoped aggregates
  (scoutIngestEntity.groupBy by platform/token, scoutReconstructionLedger.groupBy
  by status/platform/token) replace the count + status groupBy; page read and
  Q1 legacy-cursor lookup run over {coach_id, intent_id, OR: pairs}; cursor
  continuation is AND-ed; empty scope -> truthful empty page (legacy token 400
  as its lookup would). Accounting gains `unclassified`.
- scout-entities.service.ts: same pattern with the requested family; result
  gains `unclassified_staged`. The canonical-row join (entity_type == family)
  is unchanged: ScoutReconstructedEntity carries the canonical family.
- scout-cursor.ts (review A A1): two tokens of ONE platform may share an id
  space (a spec's sharedIdSpaces, or a step token beside the legacy family
  token), so two reconstructed ledger rows can tie on (source_id,
  source_platform) inside one family scope. The page order is now the
  ledger's unique key (source_id, source_platform, entity_type) in both
  readers, and the emitted cursor is a row-precise v3 token that carries the
  token (`t`). Pair-only v2 tokens minted before this commit are still
  accepted and completed by a bounded (take 2) lookup under the full scope
  predicate: no row shares the pair -> continue after the pair; exactly one
  row -> that row; two rows -> the boundary is ambiguous and the read fails
  closed with 400 `malformed cursor` (restart from the first page). Legacy
  source-only tokens resolve as before (exactly one row, now to the full
  triple). The token is bounded to the ingest entity_type length (128) so
  every valid row is encodable within SCOUT_CURSOR_MAX_LENGTH.
- scout-roster.controller.ts: the OpenAPI roster description lists
  `unclassified`.
- DTOs: additive required numbers; the roster `staged` description now says
  what it counts. Contract regenerated (byte-stable across two runs).
- Engine ledger identity, S9 joins, tenant scope (coach_id, intent_id) and the
  controllers are unchanged. No platform slug or token literal in src.

Unit specs: fake Prisma gains groupBy with real distinct-group semantics and a
generic AND/OR predicate matcher; new registry-scoped suites for both readers
(token-mapped source, legacy + mapped merge order, other-family exclusion,
unclassified counting, empty scope, boundedness); regressions for the tie:
two tokens, one platform, one source_id, limit 1 across pages -> both rows
served exactly once in a stable order, plus the v2 ambiguous / one-row /
no-row and legacy tied-id resolutions; scout-cursor.spec rewritten for v3;
fixtures updated for the two additive fields.
